{
  "image_name": "cdfaf71a517b966ca50fd0303fc75594.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes how surface curvature is defined and how common shapes are classified by two principal curvatures. On the left, a curved surface is shown with two perpendicular planes cutting through it, indicating the principal curvature directions, labeled \\(k_1\\) and \\(k_2\\). A lower schematic explains curvature as the inverse of a radius, \\(1/R\\): tighter bends have higher curvature, and broader bends have lower curvature, with convex and concave cases distinguished by sign.\n\nThe large panel organizes surfaces on axes of Gaussian curvature \\(K\\) and mean curvature \\(H\\). This map shows that spherical surfaces lie in the region with both \\(K\\) and \\(H\\) positive, meaning both principal curvatures have the same sign. Cylindrical surfaces sit on the line \\(K=0\\), where one principal curvature is zero and the other is nonzero. Minimal surfaces appear where \\(H=0\\) but \\(K<0\\), indicating opposite-signed principal curvatures of equal magnitude, as in saddle-like forms. A flat plane is highlighted at the origin, where both \\(K\\) and \\(H\\) are zero and both principal curvatures vanish. The central shaded region marked “forbidden shapes” suggests combinations of \\(K\\) and \\(H\\) that are not geometrically possible.",
